  "constraintReading": "Up to 10⁵ bars: trying every run is O(n²), about 5·10⁹ runs, far too slow. O(n) means each bar is pushed once and popped once, and the pop finds its shorter neighbor on each side; the stack can hold every index, so the space is O(n).",
  "hints": {
    "clarify": "Can a rectangle stick out above a bar it covers? No: it's capped by the shortest bar in its run. Try [3, 1, 3] by hand: each 3 alone gives 3, and the whole run gives 1 × 3 = 3, so the best is 3. In [2, 4, 4], the two 4s together give 8, better than all three bars at height 2 (6).",
    "approach": "Stack of indexes whose heights rise from bottom to top. Think of each bar as the shortest bar of its rectangle: the rectangle spreads left and right until a shorter bar stops it. Walk the bars left to right. When the new bar is no taller than the bar on top, that top bar has found its right edge: pop it. Its left edge is the bar now on top of the stack (or the start of the chart, if the stack is empty), so its width is the gap between those two edges. After the last bar, a bar of height 0 pops everything left.",
    "whyNot": "Trying every run of bars while tracking its shortest bar is O(n²): about 5·10⁹ runs for 10⁵ bars. Spreading out left and right from every bar is also O(n²) on a chart of equal bars. The stack finds both edges of every bar with one push and one pop each.",
    "slots": {
      "setup": "An empty stack of indexes; their heights rise from the bottom of the stack to the top. The best area starts at 0.",
      "loop": "Visit the bars left to right, then one extra step with a height of 0 that flushes the stack.",
      "update": "While the top bar is at least as tall as the current one, pop it. Then push the current index, so the stack keeps rising.",
      "record": "For each popped bar, the current index is its right edge and the new top of the stack its left edge: area = its height × (right − left − 1). Keep the largest.",
      "return": "After the flush step, the stack is empty and every bar has been measured: return the best area."
    }
  },
  "solution": {
    "code": "class Solution:\n    def largestRectangleArea(self, heights: list[int]) -> int:\n        stack: list[int] = []  # indexes; their heights rise from bottom to top\n        best = 0\n        for i in range(len(heights) + 1):\n            h = heights[i] if i < len(heights) else 0\n            while stack and heights[stack[-1]] >= h:\n                top = stack.pop()  # viz:pop\n                left = stack[-1] if stack else -1\n                area = heights[top] * (i - left - 1)\n                best = max(best, area)  # viz:area\n            if i < len(heights):\n                stack.append(i)  # viz:push\n        return best  # viz:done\n",
    "explanation": "Every index is pushed once and popped once, so all the work inside the `while` adds up to O(n), and the pass is O(n) time. On rising bars, nothing is popped until the end and the stack holds all n indexes: O(n) space. When bar `top` is popped, the bar below it on the stack is the nearest shorter bar on its left, and bar `i` the nearest bar on its right that is no taller, so `top` is the shortest bar of every column between them. With equal heights, the first of them gets a width that stops early, but the last one popped spans the whole run, so the best area is still found.",
    "toolkit": []
  },
